===Noun===
{{en-noun|head=[[tree]] [[lawn]]}}

# {{context|US|Midwestern|lang=en}} The [[grass]]y area between the [[sidewalk]] and the [[street]].
#* {{quote-book|title=U.S. Landscape Ordinances: An Annotated Reference Handbook| books.google.com/books?isbn=0471292761| author=D. Gail Abbey| year=1998|  passage=Detached sidewalks generally occur in residential areas and are normally separated from the curb by a '''tree lawn'''.}}

====Usage notes====
This term has significant dialectical differences, and some dialects and idiolects do not include a term for this area,<ref name="tls">[http://tls.timesonline.co.uk/article/0,,25362-1923881,00.html The triumph of slang] (dead link) <!-- probable paywall copy at http://www.timesonline.co.uk/tol/arts_and_entertainment/the_tls/Subscriber_Archive/History_Archive/article6768242.ece -->, John A. C. Greppin, 2002-02-01</ref> often using a [[circumlocution]].<ref>[http://www.slate.com/id/2086909/ Full Metal Racket: The perverse thrill of metal detecting, the world's worst hobby.], by Emily Yoffe, ''[[w:Slate.com|Slate]],'' 2003-08-18, refers to the area as “the grassy strip that runs along the street side of many D.C. sidewalks”.</ref>

